{
  "comment": "The art bake-off of issue #49. Every bird photo is a CC0 or CC BY iNaturalist photo among the Los Angeles and San Diego packs' candidates (`python -m packbuilder.bakeoff references`, then picked by hand from build/art-bakeoff/references.jpg): `reference` is the pack-art and scene-art reference, `photos` stand in for a birder's own photos (photo art). Not references: 718923160 (hazy, through glass), 696473222 (a red object over the owl's head; kept as a photo), 739414068 (the warbler's face at the frame edge). Version 2 of the plan (after the first round) takes well-lit adult references for the House Finch (705561778, not the dim 696977499) and the Western Bluebird (729326231, not 738434109): from a dim or dull reference the model painted the stereotype. Version 3 of the plan (the full-quality pass) takes typical adults the 12-photo ranking found: the sooty Black Phoebe 715715037 (the grey 732853833 gave grey pack art), an adult male Western Bluebird 729928057 (from the dull 729326231 the model invented an Eastern-like male), the Great Horned Owl 696473222 with its ear tufts showing (689760759 lost them); and `scene_reference`, the bird pasted into scenes, is a perched light-morph Red-tailed Hawk 725544855 rather than the flight shot; for the House Finch (696977499) and the Western Bluebird (738434109) it is an unobstructed bird, because twigs across their references cut holes in the cut-out. Left out by hand: 738661333 (a photographer's watermark) and 716140587 (a streaked sparrow, not a towhee). Scenes are full-resolution glasses photos (3024 x 4032) the maintainer took in Los Angeles on 2026-10-10; they are not committed. The first rounds used 360 x 640 stream frames from sightings, too blurry once zoomed for scene art at full quality. Version 4 of the plan (FLUX.3, prompts v7): every species has `length_cm`, its typical length, which sets how far scene art zooms into the glasses photo (12x for the 14.5 cm House Finch, in proportion for the others, at least 1x); every scene has `perch`, the point (fractions of the upright photo) the bird is painted at, picked by hand in the telephoto audition: the garden's lamp post, the lawn's fallen leaves and the driveway's roses. Isolating the reference bird completes what its perch hid, so `scene_reference` no longer has to be unobstructed; the plan keeps it so that this round is comparable with the last.",
